# Decisions — Prompt-Capture Hook

> Append-only, past-tense. One decision per block. Logged during /spec interview
> (iteration 1) unless noted.

# Iteration 1

## DEC-001 — v1 is a thin, deterministic capture hook (no SDK inline)
**Decision:** v1 = a Claude Code hook (shell command) that writes user prompts to
the lake via `append_entry`. Pure plumbing — no LLM call in the hook path.
**Alternatives rejected:** (b) capture+triage inline; (c) full session-harvester
with provenance chains.
**Rationale:** an inline SDK/LLM call adds latency, cost, and a failure surface to
every prompt and can't fail-open without dropping data. The intelligence
(triage/contextualize) belongs in a later async layer over the lake — which is
also the right home for the first SDK pilot.
**Context:** via /spec interview Q1 (iteration 1).

## DEC-002 — Everything goes; no promotion/triage in v1
**Decision:** the hook captures ALL user prompts. No promotion state, no
filtering, no triage in v1. Unstructured is acceptable now.
**Rationale:** the timestamped *flow* of prompts is itself valuable data for
thread-tracking (the shape of attention over time), independent of any single
prompt being a "keeper." Promotion is deferred to a later layer (candidate SDK
pilot). Keeps v1 the simplest possible build.
**Context:** via /spec interview Q3 (iteration 1).

## DEC-003 — /checkin shows hook entries (no source filter)
**Decision:** `/checkin` / `read_day` is NOT modified to skip `claude-hook`
entries. Day-based targeting is sufficient; the captured prompts double as a
"what I was working on" trace that Levi wants in the daily read.
**Alternatives rejected:** exclude `claude-hook` from checkin until a promotion
layer can surface only the good ones.
**Rationale:** Levi wants the flow read into checkin now; future pollution is
accepted as a later problem on a still-small dataset. Consequence: part 3
requires NO change to part 1's `read_day`.
**Context:** via /spec interview Q4 (iteration 1).

## DEC-004 — Promotion-state, if/when built, stays off the entry field-contract
**Decision:** when a promotion/triage layer is eventually built, its state lives
as a `tag` or a downstream/strata index — NOT as a new top-level entry field.
**Rationale:** the schema's philosophy (part 1 DEC-009) keeps lifecycle/triage
state downstream; the raw entry stays thin. Avoids reopening the keystone
field-contract. Noted now as forward design; not built in v1.
**Context:** via /spec interview Q3 (iteration 1).

## DEC-005 — Global hook scope (`~/.claude/settings.json`)
**Decision:** the hook is installed globally — it fires in every Claude Code
session, not just sessions rooted in the Collevity workspace.
**Alternatives rejected:** workspace-scoped (`00_COLLEVITY/.claude/settings.json`).
**Rationale:** Levi is still mid-migration into the Collevity workspace, so
relevant work lives outside it today. Global avoids losing that. Narrowing later
(move config down to the workspace) is reversible and lossless; widening after the
fact would lose data. Revisit once migration completes.
**Context:** via /spec interview Q5 (iteration 1).

## DEC-006 — AC tree: 5 top-level groups vs merged write-path grouping
**Decision:** AC tree uses 5 distinct top-level groups — installation (AC1),
payload correctness (AC2), persistence (AC3), fail-open (AC4), readability (AC5).
**Alternatives rejected:** merge AC1+AC2+AC3 into a single "write path" group
(alternative raised during seeding).
**Rationale:** keeping installation, payload shape, and persistence as separate
groups makes each independently testable and makes the MECE coverage explicit.
Merging them would obscure which criterion the sub-AC was actually testing.
**Context:** /spec seed structuring choice (iteration 1).

## DEC-007 — Implementation phases: 2 (build + verify) vs 1 (all together)
**Decision:** 2 phases — Phase 1 is the full build (script + wiring + fail-open),
Phase 2 is end-to-end live verification (submit prompt, inspect lake, run
read_day).
**Alternatives rejected:** single phase (all ACs together); 3 phases (install,
script, verify).
**Rationale:** Phase 2's AC5.1 requires a live running installation to exercise,
making it genuinely a later step. Single phase obscures that dependency. 3 phases
over-split an already small build (the script is ~50 lines). 2 phases is the
minimum split that reflects the real gate.
**Context:** /spec seed structuring choice (iteration 1).

## DEC-008 — Drop `seq` and `parent_id` from the reserved `claude-hook` context shape
**Decision:** Remove both `seq` and `parent_id` from the reserved `claude-hook`
`context` shape in part-1 `SCHEMA.md`. The reserved (and hook-written) shape is
now exactly `{ kind, session_id, cwd }`.
**Alternatives rejected:** (a) drop `parent_id` only, keep `seq` reserved — leaves
the same weakness unaddressed in `seq`; (b) keep `parent_id` and pin a concrete
definition (sub-agent parent-session link vs prior-prompt chain) — adds a contract
the hook does not fill.
**Rationale (Levi):** both are undefined placeholders that collide with the
keystone's thin-entry ethic (part-1 DEC-009: typing/structure accrete downstream,
never on the raw entry). A within-session sequence and a prompt-to-prompt parent
link are both **derivable** from `created_at` ordering under a `session_id` (and
`id` is time-ordered UUIDv7), so neither adds information. `parent_id` is also
largely inapplicable: `UserPromptSubmit` captures user-typed prompts, not the
programmatic prompts of sub-agent child sessions. Linkage is the strata/thread
layer's job (part 4), not the raw stream's.
**Consequences:** part-1 `SCHEMA.md` reserved shape + prose amended (cross-part
edit to a closed-iteration doc, same pattern as the cwd amendment; rationale
tracked here, not as a new part-1 DEC). Part-3 spec.md reconciled — `seq` removed
from Constraints, Out-of-scope, and AC2.6; AC2.6 now asserts `context` carries
only `kind`/`session_id`/`cwd`. Resolves the seed session's pending `parent_id`
open thread.
**Context:** resolved this session (2026-06-30), pre-`/spec review`, answering the
seed package's DECISION PENDING.

## DEC-009 — Fail-open leaves a breadcrumb: best-effort error sidecar
**Decision:** On any swallowed failure, the hook makes a best-effort append of a
diagnostic line to an error sidecar file; the sidecar write is itself wrapped so
it can never break fail-open (still exit 0, empty stdout, never exit 2). New leaf
AC4.3.
**Alternatives rejected:** (a) accept silent discard, no log — a broken hook stays
invisible until a gap is noticed at `/checkin`; (c) write diagnostics to stderr —
`UserPromptSubmit` stderr handling is version-dependent, risks either being
swallowed anyway or leaking noise into the session.
**Rationale (Levi):** fail-open is non-negotiable, but "swallow" was silently also
"discard invisibly." A sidecar turns invisible loss into one file to check when
captures seem missing, at ~3 lines of cost, without weakening fail-open.
**Context:** /spec revise (iteration 1), concern O3+C3 (two personas converged on
the observability blind spot).

## DEC-010 — AC3.1 promises one line per invocation, not per prompt; duplicates tolerated
**Decision:** Reword AC3.1 from "exactly one line per prompt" to "one line per
hook invocation," with an accepted-risk note: if `UserPromptSubmit` ever
double-fires, the resulting duplicate entry is a low-harm triage-layer concern
(DEC-002), not a hook defect. No dedup code.
**Alternatives rejected:** (a) build real idempotency (content-hash / (session_id,
text, timestamp) dedup before append) — adds read-back + state to a hook whose
identity is thin deterministic plumbing (DEC-001), the only v1 element that would
violate the everything-goes posture; (c) leave "exactly one" — a latent false
claim (fire count is Claude Code's property, not the hook's), which a verify pass
could wrongly fail the hook for.
**Rationale (Levi):** duplicates are nearly harmless in an append-only lake that
gets triaged anyway; the fix is honesty in the AC, not code.
**Context:** /spec revise (iteration 1), concern O2 (ontologist's most load-bearing).

## DEC-011 — created_at: sub-second precision at capture; offset correctness inherited
**Decision:** The hook stamps `created_at` with sub-second (microsecond) precision
(e.g. `...T14:05:00.123456-04:00`), not `:00` seconds (AC2.2). Offset *correctness*
is inherited from part-1 DEC-017 — owned at capture, not re-derived or validated
here; the hook validates format, not that the host clock is right.
**Alternatives rejected:** (a) keep second precision — collapses near-simultaneous
captures and caps the finest orderable resolution; (c) add a correctness guard
(NTP / expected-zone check) — nothing else in the system second-guesses the OS
clock, so it would be inconsistent overreach.
**Rationale (Levi):** chronological order is recovered by sorting `created_at`, so
its precision sets how finely events can be ordered; microseconds conform to
part-1's ISO-8601 validator with no schema change, and future-proof the data even
though `read_day` surfaces minute granularity today (see DEC-013). Also resolves
the offset-correctness ambiguity flagged by O1+C4 (is it a bug or a decision?) —
it is an inherited decision.
**Context:** /spec revise (iteration 1), concerns O1+C4 and Levi's precision push.

## DEC-012 — Chronological order: read-time sort + settle-time compaction (Option B)
**Decision:** The lake becomes chronological (a) on read via `read_day`'s existing
in-memory sort, and (b) physically **on settle**: `sync_sources` rewrites the lake
sorted by `created_at` (reusing part-1's atomic `_rewrite_all`). Live hook appends
stay O(1) at the file tail; only the transient tail since the last sync is
unsorted. The hook itself does no ordering beyond stamping an accurate,
high-precision `created_at`. (Physical compaction is part-1 dependency D2.)
**Alternatives rejected:** (A) sort-on-read only — keeps O(1) append but the file
on disk never becomes chronological, which Levi wants for reading the raw lake;
(C) sorted insert on every append — file always fully sorted, but every prompt
becomes an O(n) read-modify-write with much worse concurrency exposure, fighting
the append-dominant design.
**Rationale (Levi):** wants a chronological raw file to read (consistent with his
inspect-the-bytes practice) without paying per-append cost; settle-time compaction
in `sync_sources` gives both. Clarified during review that `read_day` sorts only
its returned rows, not the file — so physical ordering was genuinely unsolved.
**Context:** /spec revise (iteration 1), Levi's ordering question. Note: `id`
(UUIDv7) remains the arrival-order axis; `created_at` is the event-order axis.

## DEC-013 — read_day sort/display precision raised to sub-minute (supersedes DEC-003 consequence)
**Decision:** Part 1's `read_day` must sort and surface at sub-minute resolution so
the hook's fine-grained `created_at` (DEC-011) is actually ordered on read.
Currently it sorts by an `HH:MM` string (`lake.py`). Part-1 dependency D1.
**Supersedes:** the DEC-003 *consequence* "part 3 requires no change to part 1's
`read_day`." The source-filtering half of DEC-003 still stands (read_day is not
modified to skip `claude-hook` entries); only the "no change at all" claim is
retired.
**Alternatives rejected:** (a) store microseconds but leave read_day at minute
granularity — sub-minute order stored but never surfaced, leaving Levi's precision
requirement unmet; (c) surface raw `created_at` strings instead of formatted time
— larger change to read_day's output contract than needed.
**Rationale (Levi):** "read_day will need to be changed for precision of sorting to
a greater degree" — explicit requirement.
**Context:** /spec revise (iteration 1). Reopens part-1 (closed) for a follow-on
iteration; tracked as cross-part dependency D1.

## DEC-014 — Global scope confirmed; "lossless" claim corrected; privacy tradeoff accepted
**Decision:** Global scope (DEC-005) stands. Correct DEC-005's rationale: narrowing
scope later is lossless only *going forward* — every entry captured during the
global window (including non-Collevity / client / personal sessions) is permanent;
narrowing is **not** retroactive. The breadth-of-capture privacy tradeoff is
recorded as a knowingly accepted cost, revisit when workspace migration completes.
**Alternatives rejected:** (2) narrow to workspace scope now — kills the privacy
concern but loses the outside-workspace work that was the whole reason for global
(Levi is mid-migration); (3) global + cwd guard in the hook — that is filtering,
which DEC-002 deferred, and opens a "which roots" decision surface.
**Rationale (Levi):** confirmed capturing every session everywhere does not bother
him; the mid-migration reason for global still holds. Only the false "reversible
and lossless" wording needed fixing.
**Context:** /spec revise (iteration 1), concern C1+C2 (contrarian's biggest risk).
Amends the rationale of DEC-005 (decision unchanged).

## DEC-015 — Concurrent-append safety is a part-1 dependency, not hook work
**Decision:** The hook relies on `append_entry` being safe under concurrent
appends (global scope makes concurrent sessions realistic). Confirming — and if
needed hardening (advisory lock) — that safety is part-1's job. `append_entry`
currently does a plain `open("a")` + single `write()` with no lock (`lake.py`);
atomic enough for small appends on local disk, weaker on iCloud (DEC-022).
Part-1 dependency D3.
**Alternatives rejected:** (b) add locking inside the hook — wrong layer; every
capture surface would have to reimplement it; (c) ignore — real (if low) corruption
risk that global scope newly activates.
**Rationale (Levi):** ordering/concurrency of the shared file is a property of the
store seam, not of any one writer; keep the hook thin and push the guarantee to
`append_entry`.
**Context:** /spec revise (iteration 1), concern C7 (concurrent-append half).
Same-minute *read* collision was dismissed as a non-problem (sub-second precision
per DEC-011 + list-valued read). Reopens part-1; tracked as dependency D3.

## DEC-016 — D3 (and D1/D2) resolved by part-1 iteration 2 landing
**Decision:** All three cross-part dependencies LANDED 2026-07-11 (part-1
iteration 2, commit 078c698): D1 `read_day` sorts by parsed `created_at`
(sub-minute); D2 `sync_sources` settles the pool chronologically (atomic,
no-op when sorted); D3 all lake mutations serialize on an advisory flock
sidecar (`<pool>.lock`). The revise session's open sequencing question
(gate install on D3 / ship with disclosed gap / hook-owned lock) is moot —
D3 landed before install. Empirical evidence: 6-process × 20-append hammer
test, zero lost/torn lines. Residual accepted risk: iCloud **multi-machine**
concurrent use can still yield conflict-copy files — a sync/settle concern,
not line corruption; single-machine concurrency is now locked.
**Alternatives rejected:** (a) gate install on D3 — overtaken by events;
(c) hook-owned lock — wrong layer (reaffirms DEC-015).
**Rationale (Claude, judgment delegated by Levi 2026-07-11):** the fork built
against this spec implemented the dependencies at the layer DEC-015 assigned
them; merge + commit made the guarantee real before any install.
**Context:** /spec revise (iteration 1), resumed after the 2026-07-11 fork
merge; closes the SPP 2026-07-11-1412 open item #1.

## DEC-017 — AC4.3 error sidecar DEMOTED from AC to owned limitation
**Decision:** The best-effort error-sidecar breadcrumb stays in the
implementation (built, merged, subprocess-tested) but is no longer an
acceptance criterion. "Best-effort" is not a testable guarantee — an AC that
can fail and still pass is not an AC (contrarian). The spec now states the
breadcrumb as an implementation note under AC4 and surfaces the residual
(both writes fail → capture vanishes with zero trace) as an accepted risk in
Constraints. No sidecar lifecycle machinery (rotation, reader) is specced for v1.
**Alternatives rejected:** (a) cut entirely (simplifier) — the code is merged
and tested; deleting working observability to shorten the spec is backwards,
and count-comparison detection assumes someone is counting; (b) keep + fully
define path/format/rotation/reader (ontologist) — lifecycle machinery is
gold-plating for a v1 plumbing hook; (d) single logger call to shared debug
infra — no such shared infra exists to call.
**Rationale (Claude, judgment delegated by Levi 2026-07-11):** demotion is the
only exit that makes the spec honest without churning merged, tested code;
it adopts contrarian's framing while keeping DEC-009's discoverability goal.
**Context:** /spec revise (iteration 1), concern shared by all three personas
(review v001-2026-07-01-1638); closes SPP 2026-07-11-1412 open item #2.
